-- SCOPE, stated so the result is not over-read: this walks each candidate's DECLARED
-- dependencies at its pinned version, kind=normal and optional=false only, one level deep,
-- from https://crates.io/api/v1/crates/<name>/<version>/dependencies. For each such
-- dependency it reads the HIGHEST published, unyanked, non-prerelease version that satisfies
-- the declared requirement (Cargo's caret rule, implemented here) and prints that version's
-- declared licence — not merely the most recently published version of the crate.
-- It is NOT a resolved-lockfile audit: optional and feature-gated dependencies are not
-- followed, depth beyond one level is not walked, and no unification across the graph is
-- performed. A NOT-DUAL row therefore DISQUALIFIES a crate under an all-dependencies-dual
-- rule; an all-DUAL result does NOT qualify one.
-- The dual predicate applied here is a PROPOSAL, not an owner ruling: an SPDX expression
-- qualifies when its top-level OR arms include a bare MIT arm and an Apache-2.0 arm
-- (so 'MIT OR Apache-2.0', 'Apache-2.0 OR MIT' and 'MIT/Apache-2.0' all qualify).
